/-!
# j(qᵖ) is not a rational function of j(q)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.jqNModC_prime_not_mem_adjoin_of_charZero`
(`ca045de8-930f-52f0-8f75-75daa6a97f6b`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `d465f458-611c-54e1-b2d5-00dae6c08af8`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $p$ be a prime. Inside the field
$\operatorname{LaurentSeries} K$ of formal Laurent series over $K$, the element `jqModC K` is the
$q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant with coefficients read in $K$: it is the product of the Hahn
series $q^{-1}$ (the single term with exponent $-1$ and coefficient $1$) with the image in Laurent
series of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3\cdot\eta^{-24}$-type numerator `eisenstein4 ^ 3
* dedekindEtaUnitInv`, its integer coefficients pushed to $K$ along $\mathbb{Z}\to K$. For $N \neq
0$ the ring homomorphism `qExpand K N` is the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$, realised as the
embedding of Hahn series along multiplication by $N$ on the exponent group $\mathbb{Z}$, and
`jqNModC K N` is `qExpand K N` applied to `jqModC K`, i.e. the series $j(q^N)$. The assertion is
that `jqNModC K p`, the series $j(q^p)$, does not belong to the intermediate field of
$\operatorname{LaurentSeries} K$ over $K$ generated by the single element `jqModC K`; that is,
$j(q^p)$ is not a rational function of $j(q)$ with coefficients in $K$.

This is the base case, over an arbitrary field of characteristic zero, of the statement that
$j(q^N)$ generates a proper extension of $K(j(q))$ of degree the Dedekind $\psi$-function; the
hypothesis of characteristic zero is essential, since in characteristic $p$ one has $j(q^p)=j(q)^p$.
It is used in the construction of the function field of $X_0(N)$ in this $q$-expansion model, in
particular in establishing irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_\ell$ over $K(j(q))$ and
in the counting of poles of $j(q)$ on the resulting field.

